1: # P3K.11. Proved: the higher parameter and its two characteristic integers
2: 
3: Fix logarithmic orders \(3K,4L\), with
4: \(\gcd(3K,4L)=1\), and primitive logarithmic coefficients
5: \(n_0,n_1\), so \(\gcd(n_0,3K)=\gcd(n_1,4L)=1\).
6: For the sphere members impose
7: \(a=4L n_0+3K n_1=\pm1\), with zero additional cusp collar.
8: Use the period coordinate \(c\) and scalar \(u\) of the original
9: family.  The closed higher parameter is
10: \[
11:  w_h=\exp\!\left(-\frac{\pi i c}{KL}\right),
12:  \qquad u=\kappa w_h^{2KL},\qquad \kappa\ne0.
13: \]
14: In particular, the original expression \(w=\exp(-\pi i c)\)
15: does not give one closed higher loop when \(KL>1\).
16: The positive sixfold cover \(w_h=r^6\) gives the signed
17: gamma turn \(a\gamma\).  For the positive-gamma sphere marking
18: \(a=1\), with the descended rotating framing of P3K.9, the Jin
19: integers of the positive \(w_h\)-loop are
20: \[
21:                   (k,\ell)=(-KL,-4KL).
22: \]
23: The integer return index is zero for either sphere sign.
24: These statements identify
25: both the parameter and the framing in the supplied higher-order
26: formulas.
27: 
28: **Proof: the integral orbifold complex.** We use the marked
29: lattice and period convention of P5.6.  Its cohomological
30: monodromy matrices are
31: \[
32:  T_1=\begin{pmatrix}
33:  1&0&-6&2\\0&-1&1&1\\0&-1&0&1\\0&0&0&1
34:  \end{pmatrix},\qquad
35:  T_2=\begin{pmatrix}
36:  1&6&0&-3\\0&0&-1&1\\0&1&0&0\\0&0&0&1
37:  \end{pmatrix}.
38: \]
39: Put \(A_i=T_i^{-t}\), \(A_0=(A_1A_2)^{-1}\),
40: \(N_1=1+A_1+A_1^2\), and
41: \(N_2=1+A_2+A_2^2+A_2^3\).  The lattice local system
42: on the higher root base is the pullback of the original one.
43: Consequently its finite cyclic norm operators are exactly
44: \(K N_1\) and \(L N_2\).  This assertion retains the
45: ineffective stabilizers: the generator of the additional
46: order-\(K\) or order-\(L\) stabilizer acts as the identity
47: on the lattice, so the full cyclic sum repeats the original
48: sum precisely \(K\) or \(L\) times.
49: 
50: Here is a complete block specification of the integer complex
51: used in the computation.  Let
52: \[
53:  C^0=\Lambda\oplus\Lambda\oplus\Lambda\oplus\mathbb Z^2,
54:  \quad C^1=\Lambda^7,\quad C^2=\Lambda^5,
55:  \quad C^3=\Lambda^2,
56:  \qquad \Lambda=\mathbb Z^4,
57: \]
58: and let \(J:\mathbb Z^2\to\Lambda\) have columns
59: \(e_3,e_4\).  In the displayed ordered summands, the three
60: differentials are
61: \[
62: \begin{split}
63:  d^0(a,b,c,z)={}&\bigl((A_1-1)a,(A_2-1)a,
64:  (A_1-1)b,(A_2-1)c,\,a-b,a-c,a-Jz\bigr),\\
65:  d^1(p,q,r,s,u,v,w)={}&\bigl(KN_1r,LN_2s,
66:  p-r-(A_1-1)u,\ q-s-(A_2-1)v,\\
67:  &\hspace{39mm}-A_0p-A_0A_1q-(A_0-1)w\bigr),\\
68:  d^2(x,y,z_1,z_2,z_0)={}&\bigl((A_1-1)x,(A_2-1)y\bigr).
69: \end{split}
70: \]
71: The pair-of-pants and the three annuli give the last three
72: summands of \(C^2\).  The first two are the degree-two terms
73: of the finite cyclic resolutions; the cusp contributes its
74: invariant lattice \(J\mathbb Z^2\) in degree zero.  These
75: are precisely the gluing maps of the lattice sheaf, including
76: the cyclic degree-two terms.  In particular, no finite norm
77: relation is replaced by a rational invariant projector.
78: Direct multiplication gives \(d^1d^0=d^2d^1=0\).
79: 
80: Write
81: \[
82:  \delta=(0,0,0,1)^t,\quad
83:  \epsilon_1=(1,2,-4,0)^t,\quad
84:  \epsilon_2=(1,3,-3,0)^t.
85: \]
86: The two invariant lattices have primitive bases
87: \(B_i=(\delta,\epsilon_i)\), and the norm matrices in
88: these bases are
89: \[
90:  B_1^{-1}N_1=
91:  \begin{pmatrix}0&2&1&3\\3&0&0&0\end{pmatrix},
92:  \qquad
93:  B_2^{-1}N_2=
94:  \begin{pmatrix}0&2&2&4\\4&0&0&0\end{pmatrix}.
95: \]
96: Here \(B_i^{-1}\) denotes the inverse coordinate map on its
97: image, so these displayed expressions are integral maps
98: \(\Lambda\to\mathbb Z^2\).  A primitive basis of
99: \(\ker d^2\) is \(\operatorname{diag}(B_1,B_2,I_{12})\).
100: The exact computation eliminates twelve unit pivots in the
101: matrix of \(d^1\) in that basis.  Every operation is an
102: elementary operation over \(\mathbb Z[K,L]\); the exported
103: left and right matrices have determinant \(\pm1\).
104: The remaining matrix has the same image as the three columns
105: \[
106:  R_0=
107:  \begin{pmatrix}
108:  K&0&0\\0&0&-3K\\0&2L&0\\0&0&4L
109:  \end{pmatrix}.
110: \]
111: This last image equality is also certified integrally: the
112: export gives matrices \(C,Q\) with
113: \(R_{\rm tail}=R_0C\), \(CQ=I_3\), and
114: \(R_{\rm tail}Q=R_0\).  Thus it is a proof for symbolic
115: \(K,L\), in addition to the executed Smith forms.
116: Equivalently, adjoining the eliminated generator \(h_c\),
117: the presentation is
118: \[
119: \begin{gathered}
120:  K\delta_1=0,\qquad 2L\delta_2=0,\\
121:  3K\epsilon_1=h_c=4L\epsilon_2.
122: \end{gathered}
123: \]
124: Since \(\gcd(3K,4L)=1\), the last relation gives a free
125: rank-one group and no torsion.  Moreover \(K\) is odd and
126: \(\gcd(K,L)=1\).  Therefore
127: \[
128:  H^2(\widehat B,\mathcal H)
129:  =\mathbb Z\oplus\mathbb Z/K\oplus\mathbb Z/(2L)
130:  =\mathbb Z\oplus\mathbb Z/(2KL).
131: \]
132: The complete matrices and elementary transformations are in
133: `code/part3_fourfold/higher_parameter.json`.
134: The same script computes the full integer complex, rather than
135: only this reduced presentation, for
136: \((K,L)=(1,1),(1,2),(1,5),(3,1),(5,1),(5,2),(7,5),(13,7)\).
137: Their torsion orders are respectively
138: \(2,4,10,6,10,20,70,182\), with free rank one in every case.
139: 
140: **Proof: the actual torsor period.** In the fixed period
141: coordinates, changing \(c\) to \(c'=c-b\) is the integral
142: shear
143: \[
144:  S_b=I+bE_{41},\qquad
145:  \Pi_c=\Pi_{c'}S_b,\qquad b\in\mathbb Z.
146: \]
147: It fixes \(\delta\) and sends each \(\epsilon_i\) to
148: \(\epsilon_i+b\delta\).  The logarithmic vectors are
149: \(r_i\delta+n_i\epsilon_i\).  Their actual logarithmic
150: annulus cocycles therefore change the characteristic class
151: by
152: \[
153:              b(n_0\delta_1+n_1\delta_2).
154: \]
155: This follows either by inserting the two vectors in the
156: displayed cochain complex, or by taking the logarithms in the
157: gluing maps \(x\mapsto x+\log(s_i)v_i/(2\pi i)\).
158: The coefficient \(r_i\) is fixed by the shear and contributes
159: zero to the difference.  Primitivity implies
160: \(\gcd(n_0,K)=\gcd(n_1,2L)=1\), so the displayed torsion
161: element has order exactly \(2KL\).  A change of logarithm
162: branch changes its cochain representative by a coboundary
163: and leaves this conclusion unchanged.
164: 
165: For completeness, the zero of this characteristic obstruction
166: is realized by actual holomorphic translations.  The vector
167: bundle of Lie algebras on the higher root base is the pullback
168: of the original bundle.  The added stabilizers act trivially
169: on its fibres.  Taking their invariants is exact in
170: characteristic zero, and the coarse pushforward is unchanged.
171: Consequently the explicit original calculation
172: \(H^1(\widehat B,\mathcal V)=H^2(\widehat B,\mathcal V)=0\)
173: continues to hold.  In the exponential sequence
174: \(0\to\mathcal H\to\mathcal V\to\mathcal V/\mathcal H\to0\),
175: the connecting map from torsors to \(H^2(\mathcal H)\) is
176: therefore an isomorphism.  When \(2KL\mid b\), solve the
177: integer coboundary equation above and subtract its logarithms
178: on the overlapping charts.  The remaining vector-valued
179: cocycle is a coboundary by \(H^1(\mathcal V)=0\); exponentiating
180: its chartwise primitives gives the required translation
181: isomorphism.  Reversing these primitives is its inverse.
182: At the cusp the shear changes the bounded beta term by an
183: integer; its toric factors \(\exp(2\pi i C\lambda)\), fan,
184: and monomial transition maps are identical.  Thus these chart
185: translations extend through the actual filling, as in the
186: explicit sheaf construction of P5.6.  Conversely a marked
187: translation isomorphism makes the characteristic difference
188: zero, so \(2KL\mid b\).  This proves the asserted exact
189: period in the marked family, with an extending morphism in
190: both directions.
191: 
192: **Proof: the loop and its characteristic numbers.** The scalar
193: relation is \(u=\kappa\exp(-2\pi i c)\).  Quotienting the
194: marked parameter by its just-proved period \(2KL\) gives
195: \(w_h=\exp(-\pi i c/(KL))\), and consequently
196: \(u=\kappa w_h^{2KL}\).  Put \(d=12KL\),
197: \(u=\kappa r^d\), and \(v=\log r/(2\pi i)\).
198: We retain the sign of the actual origin calculation.  The
199: finite-cap parameter descent is translation by
200: \[
201:  H(t)\delta=-\frac{4L n_0\log t+3K n_1\log(t-1)}{2\pi i}\delta.
202: \]
203: At the cusp it is \((as+U(q))\delta\), where \(U\) is a
204: single-valued holomorphic unit logarithm.  Write the period
205: \(\gamma=(\Gamma,C-s-dv)^t\).  On \(q=Q^d\), the
206: actual extending origins are
207: \[
208: \begin{split}
209:  o&=\binom{-a\Gamma s/d}{a s^2/(2d)-aCs/d+v(as+U)},\\
210:  \sigma&=\binom{a\Gamma v}{vU+aCv-ad v^2/2+ad v/2}.
211: \end{split}
212: \]
213: Their parameter differences are respectively \(H\delta\)
214: and \(H\delta+a\gamma\).  Their \(Q\)-differences are
215: integral lattice periods because \(d\) is even.  The
216: bounded cusp expression for \(\sigma\), and the invariant
217: zero coordinate after subtracting \(o\), give precisely the
218: extending sections in the node charts used in P2.2--3.
219: This checks the same morphisms with \(12\) replaced by
220: \(d\); it retains the signed twist.  Subtracting gives
221: \[
222:  F=\sigma-o
223:  =a\binom{\Gamma(s+dv)/d}{C(s+dv)/d-(s+dv)^2/(2d)+dv/2},
224: \]
225: and hence
226: \[
227:  F(s,v+\theta)-F(s,v)
228:  =a\theta\gamma+\frac{ad}{2}\theta(1-\theta)\delta.
229: \]
230: The last term is a periodic unitary gauge with both endpoint
231: values zero.  Thus the quotient clutch is the signed gamma
232: loop \(a\gamma\).  Equating the scalar expressions gives
233: the exact sixfold map \(w_h=r^6\), independently of the sign.
234: 
235: For \(a=1\), the page calculation P3F.17 gives
236: \[
237:  s_\delta=-2KL,\qquad p_{\rm rel}=-24KL.
238: \]
239: The normal-frame calculation of P3K.9 applies with page degree
240: \(12KL\).  Its transition factors into a two-dimensional
241: factor and a circle factor, exactly as in that proof;
242: the cubic clutch integral remains zero.  Thus
243: \(p_1(\bar\nu)=0\), whereas the Euler determinant has
244: square \(b^2=4s_\delta=-8KL\).  The oriented sphere-bundle
245: comparison P3K.7 gives
246: \[
247:  k_\gamma=\frac{3(-8KL)}4=-6KL,
248:  \qquad \ell_\gamma=-24KL.
249: \]
250: To descend the framing, choose any framing over the
251: \(w_h\)-circle.  Its sixfold pullback differs from the
252: rotating framing by a class in
253: \(\pi_1\operatorname{Map}(S^2,SO(3))=\mathbb Z/2\oplus\mathbb Z\).
254: The free difference is \(-6KL-6k_0\), which is divisible
255: by six.  Remove the constant-axis torsion loop, which extends
256: over the fixed \(D^3\) and changes neither characteristic
257: number.  The remaining class is the sixfold pullback of an
258: integral framing class, exactly as proved in P3K.9.  The
259: resulting descended rotating framing therefore gives
260: \(6k=-6KL\) and \(6\ell=-24KL\).  This proves the
261: stated pair and \(A=(4k-\ell)/24=0\).  For \(a=-1\),
262: the just-computed clutch traverses the gamma loop inversely.
263: The actual real trace of P3K.6--7 applies to this source
264: page with the corresponding boundary orientation; the index
265: of the inverse loop is the negative of the forward index.
266: That forward index is zero since
267: \(12s_\delta-p_{\rm rel}=0\).  Consequently the covered
268: index is zero also for \(a=-1\), and integral additivity
269: implies that the original \(w_h\)-loop has index zero.
270: The displayed individual pair is assigned to the stated
271: positive-gamma marking, so it involves no implicit reversal
272: of a negative sphere datum.
273: 
274: The original expression \(w\) and the higher expression
275: \(w_h\) satisfy \(w=w_h^{KL}\).  A single positive
276: \(w\)-loop only advances \(c\) by its original period two;
277: the higher characteristic obstruction vanishes for that
278: advance exactly when \(KL=1\).  This explicitly accounts
279: for the otherwise different-looking cover counts.  Changing
280: the final normal framing still changes \((k,\ell)\) by
281: \(n(1,4)\), as in P3F.11; the displayed values use the
282: descended rotating convention throughout.  **End of proof.**
